{"clips": [
  {
    "id": "C1",
    "weakest_point": "The first 3 seconds are only a salute and a request for a report; a scroller has no reason to care until the informant explains the takeover at 5 s. The ad also ends on a confident vow rather than a peak of danger.",
    "hook": {
      "score": 2,
      "reason": "Above the 3-s line the scroller only gets 'Your Grace' and 'Tell me what is happening in Ashburg'—a greeting and a question that need context. The strong beat (the informant's report) arrives after 3 s."
    },
    "cliffhanger": {
      "score": 3,
      "reason": "It ends on Alexander's line 'Then Ashburg will remember who it belongs to,' which opens a question but eases tension into resolve rather than cutting on an unanswered peak."
    },
    "questions": [
      {"q": "Who is Lord Sterling and is he the same person as Rupert?", "type": "confusion"},
      {"q": "Will Alexander reclaim Ashburg and free his mother?", "type": "intrigue"},
      {"q": "What proof are they waiting for?", "type": "intrigue"}
    ],
    "standalone": {
      "score": 3,
      "reason": "Followable, but rapid name-dropping (Sterling, Rupert, Catherine) and their relationships require a moment to parse before the stakes are fully clear."
    },
    "emotion": {
      "score": 4,
      "reason": "The trembling fist and the line 'My mother is a prisoner in her own house' convey real anguish and simmering rage."
    },
    "want_next": {
      "score": 3,
      "reason": "Interesting setup, but the ending is a declaration of intent rather than a sharp, urgent peak."
    },
    "ad_policy": {
      "level": "none",
      "category": "none",
      "reason": "No on-screen violence, blood, or sexual content; only a clenched fist and verbal threats."
    },
    "one_line_verdict": "A slow-burn intelligence report that builds rage but opens soft and closes on a vow."
  },
  {
    "id": "C2",
    "weakest_point": "The mid-ad cut from Alexander's forest vow to the villains' tea party is slightly abrupt, and the informant's line 'And when we have the proof?' is the only beat that needs a second of context.",
    "hook": {
      "score": 5,
      "reason": "It opens on the cruel, shocking line 'My mother is a prisoner in her own house' with zero context needed—an instant stake that stops the scroll."
    },
    "cliffhanger": {
      "score": 4,
      "reason": "It cuts on Rupert's sinister confession 'Not through the winter. I've ensured her quiet comfort,' leaving the mother's survival hanging, though the scene itself is calm and confident rather than visibly explosive."
    },
    "questions": [
      {"q": "Will the Dowager survive the winter?", "type": "intrigue"},
      {"q": "Will Alexander uncover the plot before they kill her?", "type": "intrigue"},
      {"q": "What proof do Alexander and the informant need?", "type": "intrigue"}
    ],
    "standalone": {
      "score": 5,
      "reason": "Immediately clear who is wronged (the imprisoned Dowager), by whom (Rupert and Catherine), and what is at stake (her life and the estate), with Alexander hidden and presumed dead."
    },
    "emotion": {
      "score": 4,
      "reason": "The villains' smug discussion of murdering the Dowager creates strong anger and fear for the mother."
    },
    "want_next": {
      "score": 4,
      "reason": "The murder plot and Alexander's hidden return make the next episode feel urgent."
    },
    "ad_policy": {
      "level": "none",
      "category": "none",
      "reason": "Only verbal discussion of declining health and implied poisoning; nothing graphic or physical on screen."
    },
    "one_line_verdict": "A tight two-hander that front-loads the shock and ends on the villains' murder plot."
  }
],
  "best": "C2",
  "best_why": "C2 opens with an instant cruelty hook and ends on the villains' confession of murder, giving it far higher scroll-stopping power and a sharper unresolved peak than C1's slower report-and-vow structure."
}
